DEEP = [
T("More math operators",
  "Besides `+ - * /`, Python has three more math operators you'll use constantly. `//` is **floor division**: it divides and throws away the fraction. `%` (modulo) gives the **remainder** left over after dividing. `**` raises a number to a **power**.",
  "print(7 / 2)    # 3.5  normal division\nprint(7 // 2)   # 3    whole times 2 fits into 7\nprint(7 % 2)    # 1    what's left over\nprint(2 ** 10)  # 1024 2 multiplied by itself 10 times",
  "`//` and `%` are two halves of the same division: 7 is 2 × 3 with 1 left over, so `7 // 2` is 3 and `7 % 2` is 1. `**` repeats multiplication, so `2 ** 3` is 2 × 2 × 2.",
  "Splitting things into groups (how many full boxes of 12 eggs), checking even or odd (`n % 2` is 0 for even numbers), turning seconds into minutes, and growth calculations like interest."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["3", "2", "3.4"], "2",
  "17 divided by 5 is 3 with 2 left over. `%` gives the leftover, so the answer is 2.",
  code="print(17 % 5)",
  feedback={"3": "3 is `17 // 5`, the number of whole fives. `%` gives what's left over.", "3.4": "3.4 is `17 / 5`, normal division."}),
C("Uptime in hours and minutes",
  "A server has been up for 135 minutes. How many whole hours is that, and how many minutes are left over? Print the hours on one line and the leftover minutes on the next.",
  "# uptime: 135 minutes. Print the whole hours, then the leftover minutes\n",
  "print(135 // 60)\nprint(135 % 60)",
  "There are 60 minutes in an hour.\nprint(135 // 60)  # whole hours\nprint(135 % 60)   # minutes left over",
  [("Floor division counts the whole groups", "`//` tells you how many whole groups fit, and `%` tells you what's left. To split 50 log lines into pages of 12: `50 // 12` full pages and `50 % 12` lines on the last one.",
    "print(50 // 12)  # 4 full pages\nprint(50 % 12)   # 2 lines left over"),
   ("Two prints, same two numbers", "Both lines use 135 and 60. The first line uses `//` (whole hours), the second uses `%` (minutes left). Each `print()` puts its result on its own line.",
    "print(100 // 60)  # 1 hour\nprint(100 % 60)   # 40 minutes")]),
T("Order of operations",
  "When an expression has several operators, Python follows math rules: `**` first, then `* / // %`, then `+ -`. Operators at the same level run left to right. **Parentheses** always go first, so use them whenever the order matters or isn't obvious.",
  "print(2 + 3 * 4)     # 14: multiply first\nprint((2 + 3) * 4)   # 20: parentheses first\nprint(10 - 4 - 3)    # 3:  left to right\nprint(2 * 3 ** 2)    # 18: power before multiply",
  "Python builds the answer in rounds: it works out the highest-priority pieces, replaces them with their results, and repeats until one value is left. `2 + 3 * 4` becomes `2 + 12`, then `14`.",
  "Getting formulas right: averages, prices with tax, unit conversions. A missing pair of parentheses is one of the most common real bugs, and a favorite interview trick question."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["24", "4", "7"], "4",
  "Multiplication happens before subtraction: `2 * 3` is 6, then `10 - 6` is 4.",
  code="print(10 - 2 * 3)",
  feedback={"24": "That's `(10 - 2) * 3`. Without parentheses, `*` runs first."}),
T("print() with several values",
  "Give `print()` several values separated by commas and it prints them all on one line with a space between. Two optional settings change that: `sep=` sets what goes **between** values, and `end=` sets what goes **after** the last one (normally a new line).",
  "print('Total:', 42)                 # Total: 42\nprint('2025', '07', '04', sep='/')  # 2025/07/04\nprint('Loading', end='...')\nprint('done')                       # Loading...done",
  "`print` turns each value into text, joins them with `sep` (a space unless you say otherwise), and adds `end` (a newline unless you say otherwise). Numbers don't need converting first.",
  "Labeled output like `Score: 10`, building dates and times from parts, and writing several pieces on one line, such as a progress message."),
C("Print a date",
  "Use a single `print()` with three values and `sep` to print: 2025-07-04",
  "# Print 2025-07-04 with one print() and sep\n",
  "print('2025', '07', '04', sep='-')",
  "Pass the three parts as separate values and set the separator to a dash:\nprint('2025', '07', '04', sep='-')",
  [("sep goes between values", "`sep` is the glue between the values you pass. With `sep=':'`, the values 10, 30 and 00 print as a clock time.",
    "print('10', '30', '00', sep=':')  # 10:30:00"),
   ("Why '07' is in quotes", "The number `07` isn't allowed in Python (no leading zeros), and `7` would print without the zero. Writing the month as the string `'07'` keeps the zero. Pass year, month and day as three separate values, then `sep=` with a dash in quotes.",
    "print('1999', '12', '31', sep='.')  # 1999.12.31")]),
T("Escape characters",
  "A backslash `\\` inside a string starts an **escape**: a two-character code for something hard to type. `\\n` is a new line, `\\t` is a tab, `\\'` and `\\\"` are quote marks that don't end the string, and `\\\\` is one real backslash.",
  "print('Line 1\\nLine 2')       # two lines\nprint('Name:\\tAda')          # tab gap\nprint('It\\'s fine')           # It's fine\nprint(\"She said \\\"hi\\\"\")      # She said \"hi\"\nprint('C:\\\\Users')             # C:\\Users",
  "When Python reads a string, a backslash means \"the next character is special\". It swaps the pair for the real character, so `\\n` becomes one invisible newline character, not two letters.",
  "Multi-line messages, lining up columns with tabs, putting quotes inside text, and Windows file paths."),
Q("Which line prints It's mine without an error?", ["print('It's mine')", "print('It\\'s mine')", "print(It's mine)"], "print('It\\'s mine')",
  "`\\'` is an escaped quote, so it's part of the text instead of ending the string. In the first option the string ends at `It`, which is a SyntaxError. (Switching to double quotes, `print(\"It's mine\")`, also works.)",
  feedback={"print('It's mine')": "The `'` in `It's` closes the string early, so Python sees leftover text and raises a SyntaxError."}),
C("Two lines, one print",
  "Use one `print()` with `\\n` to print `Host: web-01` and `Status: online` on two separate lines.",
  "# One print() that shows two lines\n",
  "print('Host: web-01\\nStatus: online')",
  "Put \\n where the line should break:\nprint('Host: web-01\\nStatus: online')",
  [("\\n is a line break inside the text", "Everything after `\\n` starts on a new line. It sits inside the quotes like any other character.",
    "print('Roses are red\\nViolets are blue')"),
   ("Write the two lines, then join them", "Write the first line's text, then `\\n`, then the second line's text, all inside one pair of quotes. Don't add a space after `\\n`, or the second line starts with a space.",
    "print('Top\\nBottom')")]),
T("Repeating text, and mixing text with numbers",
  "`*` with a string and a whole number repeats the string: `'-' * 10` is ten dashes. But `+` only joins two strings: `'Age: ' + 5` raises a **TypeError**, because Python won't guess whether you meant text or math. Convert the number first with `str()`: `'Age: ' + str(5)`.",
  "print('-' * 12)            # ------------\nprint('ha' * 3)            # hahaha\nprint('Age: ' + str(5))    # Age: 5\n# print('Age: ' + 5)       # TypeError",
  "Operators depend on types. `str * int` means repeat; `str + str` means join; `str + int` has no meaning, so Python stops with a TypeError. `str(5)` builds the text '5' from the number 5.",
  "Dividers and simple text layouts, and building messages that include numbers, like scores or prices."),
Q("What does this code print?", ["9", "333", "3 3 3"], "333",
  "`'3'` is a string because of the quotes, so `* 3` repeats it three times. Without quotes, `3 * 3` would be 9.",
  code="print('3' * 3)"),
C("Fix the TypeError",
  "This alert message crashes with a TypeError. Fix it so it prints: Failed logins: 10",
  "print('Failed logins: ' + 10)",
  "print('Failed logins: ' + str(10))",
  "Convert the number to text before joining:\nprint('Failed logins: ' + str(10))",
  [("+ needs two strings", "Joining with `+` only works when both sides are strings. `str()` turns a number into a string so the join works.",
    "print('Level ' + str(3))  # Level 3"),
   ("Change one piece", "Keep `'Failed logins: '` as it is. Wrap the number in `str( )` so the right side becomes `str(10)`. Another fix is `print('Failed logins:', 10)`, since print adds the space for you.",
    "print('Open ports: ' + str(2))")]),
T("Reading a traceback",
  "When a program crashes, Python prints a **traceback**. Read it from the bottom: the last line names the error type and gives a short message, and the line above shows the code that failed and its line number. Lines before the crash already ran.",
  "print('start')\nprint(10 / 0)\n# start\n# Traceback (most recent call last):\n#   File \"main.py\", line 2, in <module>\n#     print(10 / 0)\n# ZeroDivisionError: division by zero",
  "Python stops at the first line it can't run and reports where it was. The error type (ZeroDivisionError, TypeError, SyntaxError...) tells you what kind of problem it is; the message gives details.",
  "Debugging every program you'll ever write. Searching the last line of a traceback online is how most developers start fixing an unfamiliar error."),
Q("In a traceback, where do you find the error type and its message?", ["On the first line", "On the last line", "It isn't shown"], "On the last line",
  "The last line reads like `ZeroDivisionError: division by zero`. The lines above it show where in the code it happened."),
T("Floats aren't exact",
  "Computers store floats in binary, and many decimals (like 0.1) can't be stored exactly, the same way 1/3 can't be written exactly in decimal. So `0.1 + 0.2` prints `0.30000000000000004`. The built-in `round(number, digits)` rounds for display.",
  "print(0.1 + 0.2)             # 0.30000000000000004\nprint(round(0.1 + 0.2, 2))   # 0.3\nprint(round(3.14159, 3))     # 3.142",
  "A float holds the closest binary value it can, which is off by a tiny amount. Usually the error is invisible, but adding values can make it show up in the last digits. `round` gives back a float rounded to the number of decimal places you ask for.",
  "Anything with decimals: prices, measurements, scientific values. Real banking code avoids floats for money and counts whole cents instead."),
C("Clean up a float",
  "Two CPU load readings, 1.1 and 2.2, add up to 3.3000000000000003. Print the total rounded to 1 decimal place so it shows 3.3",
  "print(1.1 + 2.2)",
  "print(round(1.1 + 2.2, 1))",
  "Wrap the sum in round() with 1 as the number of places:\nprint(round(1.1 + 2.2, 1))",
  [("round(value, places)", "`round` takes the value first and the number of decimal places second, separated by a comma.",
    "print(round(2.675, 1))  # 2.7\nprint(round(9.87654, 2))  # 9.88"),
   ("Wrap, don't rewrite", "Keep `1.1 + 2.2` as it is and put it inside `round( , 1)`, then put that inside `print()`. That's three layers: print, round, then the sum. Count the parentheses: two open, two close.",
    "print(round(0.7 + 0.1, 1))  # 0.8")]),
]

